   "CHARACTER SHEET - MouseKit   (bake this FIRST, before any scene)\n\n🔴 THE SHEET IS PAINTED IN THE SAME MEDIUM AS THE BOOK. Opaque grey and pale gouache with soft\n  pastel, laid on a near-black board #16181C. The board is NOT covered - leave it as the\n  background of the sheet. Brush and pastel grain must be visible in the fur, some contours must\n  be left open where the pale paint simply stops, and a little black must show through the thin\n  passes. Do NOT render this animal smoothly just because there is no scenery behind it.\n  There is NO red and NO pink anywhere on this sheet.\n\nFACE: a small blunt muzzle with a rounded nose. Eyes are DRAWN - a small dark almond #14161A\n  with a SEPARATE eyebrow stroke above, set wide and low on a PALE muzzle #D8CFC0, so the eye\n  reads at thumbnail size against a black page. It must be able to be:\n    wide and staring straight up (page 1)\n    squeezed tightly shut (pages 2 and 6)\n    open with the mouth just parted, nose tipped up to smell (page 5)\n    open wide with BOTH FOREPAWS CLAMPED OVER THE EARS, mouth open in a grin (page 11)\n  Whiskers are four fine strokes that stand STRAIGHT UP when the mouse is smelling something.\n  No dot eyes, no blush, no highlight dot, no glossy catchlight.\nBODY: warm grey-brown fur #9A8F82 laid as one soft mass. 🔴 THE BELLY, THE FRONT OF THE MUZZLE\n  AND THE INSIDES OF THE EARS ARE PALE #D8CFC0 AND STAY PALE WHETHER OR NOT LIGHT IS ON THEM -\n  this is the book's value rule and it is what makes a tiny animal findable when nine tenths of\n  the page is bare black board. Do not achieve it with a rim light or a glow.\n  Ears large and round, set high. Round back, no waist.\nFOREPAWS: small and clearly separated into three fingers-worth of shape, because 🔴 THEY MUST\n  READ AS TWO DISTINCT PAWS when they are pressed over the ears on pages 10 and 11, with the\n  pink feather squeezed between paw and ear. Draw a version pressed flat over both ears and a\n  version holding a single feather against the chest.\n🔴 TAIL - AN ACTING INSTRUMENT: longer than the body, one tapering stroke that swells at the\n  root. Three states to draw on this sheet:\n    tip trembling in fine ticks (frightened, page 1)\n    wound once right around the whole body (page 6)\n    lying loose and slightly curled at the side (page 13)\n🔴 SIZE ANCHOR - draw these two comparisons on this sheet, because they are fixed for thirteen\n  pages and if they are not settled here they will never be right in a scene:\n    (a) the mouse standing on its hind legs beside ONE STAIR TREAD - the tread is TWICE the\n        mouse's full standing height, so the tread's front edge is above its head;\n    (b) the mouse standing beside the THICK BELL ROPE lying against a wall - the rope is as\n        thick as the mouse's own body.\nBUILD & SILHOUETTE: a small young mouse, about two heads tall on all fours, rising onto its hind\n  legs only when it uses its forepaws. Silhouette = a soft comma with two round ears and a long\n  thin tail, and 🔴 two pale patches (muzzle, belly) that survive at thumbnail size.\nREFERENCE SHEET: full body side view on all fours / standing on hind legs looking straight up,\n  both forepaws pressed together at the chest, tail tip trembling / belly pressed flat to a\n  stair tread, legs splayed, head just lifted / standing on hind legs with the nose touching a\n  rope and one forepaw braced on it / curled with both forepaws over the ears and eyes shut /\n  sitting with both forepaws holding a feather against the chest, tail loose;\n  plus THREE face close-ups: eyes wide and staring up / eyes squeezed shut / eyes wide open with\n  both forepaws over the ears and the mouth open in a grin.\n  Plain near-black board background, no scenery, no red, no pink.\nFACE SEPARATION (required): the face must read apart from the body in VALUE - the pale muzzle\n  and inner ears carry it, never one dark mass. Test: shrink this sheet to thumbnail size and the\n  mood must still be legible. This adds no colour.\nANTHROPOMORPHISM GRADE: a quadruped. Rising onto the hind legs to use the forepaws is the\n  maximum. No hands, no fingers, no clothes.\nSCENE token: MouseKit.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - MouseSister   (bake SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\n🔴 SAME MEDIUM AS ABOVE - opaque grey and pale gouache on near-black board, grain visible,\n  contours left open where the pale paint stops. No red, no pink on this sheet.\n\n🔴 SHE IS ALMOST NEVER SEEN. For the first nine pages she is a voice coming down the stair from\n  above and she is OUT OF FRAME. She appears in the picture only at the bell chamber and after.\n  So this sheet exists for exactly two jobs: to be instantly distinguishable from MouseKit, and\n  to be able to WRAP HER TAIL AROUND HIM.\n\nFACE: same drawn eye and separate eyebrow stroke as MouseKit, but the eye is narrower and often\n  half-lidded and calm. Muzzle a little longer. She must be able to: tilt her face down toward\n  her brother while her own ears are laid half back / look out of a window with her eyes\n  narrowed contentedly.\nBODY: fur one clear step DARKER than her brother's - #7E7468 - and 🔴 her pale belly patch is\n  NARROWER than his, so when the two are in one frame the reader tells them apart by HOW MUCH\n  PALE EACH ONE HAS. Ears large but set slightly lower.\nBUILD & SILHOUETTE: about one and a half times MouseKit in every dimension, longer in the back,\n  and 🔴 HER TAIL IS NOTICEABLY THICKER AND LONGER THAN HIS - that tail is the only thing she\n  does in this book.\n🔴 REQUIRED PAIR DRAWING ON THIS SHEET: the two mice side by side on a stair, she on all fours\n  with her LONG TAIL LOOPED AROUND HIS BACK while he crouches with both forepaws over his ears.\n  Settle that two-body silhouette here. She does NOT pick him up, does NOT put a paw around him\n  and does NOT speak in that pose - the tail is the whole gesture.\nREFERENCE SHEET: full body side view on all fours / same, with the tail curled forward in a loop\n  / the required pair drawing above / a close-up of her face tilted down and to one side with\n  the ears half back / a size comparison standing beside MouseKit.\n  Plain near-black board background, no scenery.\nANTHROPOMORPHISM GRADE: a quadruped throughout. No hands, no fingers, no clothes, never upright.\nSCENE token: MouseSister. 🔴 Never write \"Mouse\" alone, and never let the two tokens be swapped.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - TowerPigeon   (bake THIRD, attach as @image3)\n\n🔴 SAME MEDIUM AS ABOVE - opaque paint on near-black board. No red, no pink except that ONE\n  feather is dusty pink #D9B4AE.\n🔴 A REAL PIGEON. It does not speak, does not smile, has no eyebrows and never gestures with a\n  wing. It is one of the things that are actually in a bell tower.\n\nBODY: a common town pigeon - grey-blue #7C8A90 body, a slightly darker head, two dark bars\n  across the folded wing, a coral-dark beak and feet kept low in value so they read as dark, not\n  as a colour accent. Its dusty-pink #D9B4AE breast-side feathers are where the one loose feather\n  comes from.\n🔴 TWO STATES, DRAWN SIDE BY SIDE ON THIS SHEET, because they are the same bird on two facing\n  pages and the reader must believe that:\n  STATE A - BACKLIT BURST (page 6): seen against a window, so it is a BLACK SILHOUETTE and\n    nothing inside it is described. Both wings thrown to their widest, feet kicking off the sill,\n    body CROPPED by the top of the frame. 🔴 The whole bird is never visible in this state - that\n    is the point of it. Loose feathers scatter as small pale shapes in the light around it.\n  STATE B - SETTLED AND LIT (page 7): standing on the sill in full window light with the entire\n    body visible, breast puffed up, neck pulled in, head cocked so ONE round eye looks sideways\n    and down at the mouse. Wings folded, tail out past the sill.\n🔴 SIZE ANCHOR - draw this comparison on the sheet: the settled pigeon and MouseKit standing on\n  the same lit stone in the same light. The pigeon is SEVERAL TIMES the mouse.\nREFERENCE SHEET: state A / state B / a close-up of the cocked head with one eye / the nest of dry\n  grass and twigs on a stone sill, with two feathers lying in it / ONE loose dusty-pink feather\n  drawn alone at large scale, shaft and barbs.\n  Plain near-black board background.\nANTHROPOMORPHISM GRADE: a real bird. It never speaks.\nSCENE tokens: TowerPigeon, PinkFeather. Never write \"bird\" alone.",
   "PROP SHEET - TowerProps   (bake FOURTH, attach as @image4)\n\n🔴 SAME MEDIUM AS ABOVE - opaque paint on near-black board, grain visible. These four objects\n  appear on nearly every page and they must be the SAME objects every time.\n\nBELL ROPE (BellRope): a thick hemp rope as thick as MouseKit's own body, the twist stated by five\n  or six short diagonal pale strokes running around it and a few loose fibres flicking out.\n  Draw it in three conditions: hanging slack against the wall / COILED on the floor with the end\n  lying loose (its page-1 and page-13 state) / pulled taut and whipping. 🔴 It is one continuous\n  rope from the floor coil to the bell - the same rope, never two different ropes.\nBRASS BELL (BrassBell): a big church bell in a heavy timber frame with iron straps. Brass #B08A3C\n  as ONE flat matte plane, slightly mottled with age, and ONE pale stroke along the lower rim -\n  that stroke is the entire shine. 🔴 No mirror, no chrome, no environment reflection, no\n  rendered metal. Draw it three ways: the LOWER RIM ALONE as a thumbnail-sized chip seen far\n  above (its page-5 state) / the whole bell hanging still, cropped by the top of the frame /\n  swung over to one side with the clapper just struck.\n  🔴 Draw a size comparison: MouseKit standing beside the bell's lower rim. The bell overflows\n  the frame - the mouse is a fraction of the rim alone.\nSTAIR UNIT (StairStone): three wedge-shaped treads of one spiral flight, hollowed in the middle\n  from wear, with the low finger-thick stone kerb on the inner edge and rough coursed stone\n  outside. 🔴 Draw the SAME three treads twice: once seen FROM BELOW (only the undersides, the\n  soffits, overlapping) and once seen FROM ABOVE (only the walking surfaces). These two drawings\n  are the seed of pages 1 and 12 and must be settled here.\nNEST AND SILL (WindowSill): a tall narrow arrow slit with its inner sill cut on a slope, dust on\n  the stone, a loose nest of dry grass and twigs, and pigeon footprints in the dust.\nREFERENCE SHEET: all four laid out with MouseKit's silhouette beside each at correct relative\n  scale. Plain near-black board background, no scenery.\nSCENE tokens: BellRope, BrassBell, StairStone, WindowSill."
